Retro Visualizer - NOTICE

Independent fan project, inspired by the classic Windows Media Player visualizers. Not affiliated with,
endorsed or sponsored by Microsoft. Windows and Windows Media are trademarks of the Microsoft group of
companies; all other trademarks belong to their owners. They are used here only to say which files the
optional classic loader reads and what the project is compatible with.

WHAT IS INCLUDED
- Our own code (MIT, see LICENSE): two visualizer engines written from behavioural specifications, a
  music panel, an audio analyser, a classic-preset loader, an embeddable build.
- Our own content: 12 plasma presets (own recipes and palettes), 6 spectrum styles, a synthetic demo beat.
- Level tables of the spectrum engine are computed from formulas (10^(8b/255) and 31.875*log10(x));
  dist/test checks they are bit-identical to the reference.

WHAT IS NOT INCLUDED, AND WHY
- The 25 stock plasma presets and their palettes, the 4 stock spectrum styles and their names: they are
  Microsoft's data (stored in wmp.dll and wmploc.dll.mui). Users who own Windows can load them from their
  own copy with "Load classic presets from your Windows" (read locally in the browser; nothing is uploaded).
  The loader contains no values taken from those files: it parses the DLL's registration script resource
  and interprets the code that applies the styles, at load time.
- The sample MIDI songs of C:\Windows\Media and the gm.dls sound bank: Microsoft content. The player lets
  users pick their own copies; they stay on the user's device.
- No Microsoft binaries (DLL, MUI, EXE) are distributed, and the project never links to DLL download sites.
  On a Mac/Linux/TV box, copy the files (or an exported classic pack) from a Windows PC you own.

THIRD-PARTY NOTE (to confirm before publishing)
- web/battery/shifts.js contains bit-exact ports of the x64 C runtime's sin/cos/atan2 (needed for pixel-exact
  output). Its atan2 uses two 241-entry tables of atan(j/256): the lead values are reproducible by formula
  (atan(j/256) rounded to double, low 32 bits cleared); the tail values are residuals that could not be
  regenerated bit-exactly. The algorithm and table layout match AMD's open-source LibM atan2 (BSD-3-Clause),
  which is believed to be their origin - TO VERIFY before publishing (then credit AMD here), or replace the
  tails by correctly rounded residuals (results could then differ by 1 ulp in rare cases).

Third-party code: the atan2 lookup tables are from AMD LibM (aocl-libm-ose), BSD 3-Clause.
